content:
{
  "summary": "Microsoft Defender on this computer is effectively not protecting it right now: real-time protection is off, tamper protection is off, and the entire C: drive is excluded from scanning. Together these mean almost nothing on the computer is being checked for malware, and settings like these are sometimes changed by malware itself. Look at the Defender findings first and follow the fixes shown in the report; then confirm you recognize the extra administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Defender is switched off and skipping the whole C: drive",
      "finding_ids": [
        "DEF-EXCLUSION:c2d1c54c",
        "DEF-REALTIME-OFF:6ed445bc",
        "DEF-TAMPER-OFF:1a04a8d2"
      ],
      "explanation": "These three settings work together. Real-time protection, which blocks malware as it arrives, is off. An exclusion tells Defender never to scan C:\\, which is the main drive where Windows, your programs and your files live, so even manual scans would skip almost everything. Tamper protection, which stops other programs from changing Defender's settings, is also off, so nothing prevented these changes. Defender's virus definitions are up to date (0 days old), but that doesn't help when it isn't looking. If you didn't make these changes yourself on purpose, treat this as a warning sign: switching off protection and excluding the whole drive is a known tactic malware uses to hide. Please review and apply the fixes listed in the report for each of these findings, starting with removing the C:\\ exclusion and turning real-time protection back on."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "An extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> has full administrator rights on this computer. Having a separate administrator account is often normal and even good practice, so this alone is not a problem. Just make sure it's an account you or someone in your household set up. Given that Defender's protections were turned off, it's worth taking a moment to confirm you recognize it; if you don't, see the report's guidance for this finding."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "DEF-TAMPER-OFF:1a04a8d2",
      "suggested_severity": "high",
      "reason": "On this computer tamper protection being off coincides with real-time protection disabled and a whole-drive exclusion, the exact kind of changes tamper protection is meant to prevent, so it matters more here than it would on its own."
    }
  ]
}
